<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Penn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1644">1644</a>–<a href="/years/1718">1718</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Penn was an English Quaker and the founder of the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>. Son of the admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sir-william-penn">Sir William Penn</a>, he became a trustee of West Jersey and a proprietor of East Jersey, and in 1681 Charles II granted him a vast colony west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a> to settle a debt owed to his father. He planned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> and guaranteed religious freedom; in 1682 he also received the Lower Counties that became Delaware.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-penn.jpg" alt="William Penn" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Penn.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>William Penn</b> reached the Delaware River aboard the Welcome to take possession of his new colony.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-27">27 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1682 James granted the three counties of New Castle, Kent and Sussex to <b>William Penn</b>, who governed them with the Province of Pennsylvania.</blockquote><a href="/state/delaware-colony">Delaware Colony</a></li><li><blockquote>After Carteret's death his heirs sold the colony in 1682 to 24 proprietors, many of them Quakers and Scots, including <b>William Penn</b> and Robert Barclay.</blockquote><a href="/state/east-jersey">East Jersey</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1681 Charles II granted <b>William Penn</b> a vast tract west of the Delaware River to settle a debt owed to Penn's father.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-pennsylvania">Province of Pennsylvania</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1674 Lord Berkeley sold his half of New Jersey to the Quakers John Fenwick and Edward Byllynge, and <b>William Penn</b> became a trustee for the debt-ridden Byllynge.</blockquote><a href="/state/west-jersey">West Jersey</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Ruscombe, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1692" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4547 381 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of William Penn"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 651h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1692">What was here in 1692? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Certainly, if the Common Law be so hard to be understood, it's far from being very Common…&#8221;</p><footer>At his trial at the Old Bailey, September 1670 &#183; original &#183; as the printed account of the trial (1670) records it &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7360/pg7360-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Governments, like clocks, go from the motion men give them; and as governments are made and moved by men, so by them they are ruined too. Wherefore governments rather depend upon men, than men upon governments.&#8221;</p><footer>Preface to the Frame of Government of Pennsylvania (1682) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/17th_century/pa04.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Let men be good, and the government cannot be bad; if it be ill, they will cure it. But, if men be bad, let the government be never so good, they will endeavor to warp and spoil it to their turn.&#8221;</p><footer>Preface to the Frame of Government of Pennsylvania (1682) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/17th_century/pa04.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Any government is free to the people under it (whatever be the frame) where the laws rule, and the people are a party to those laws, and more than this is tyranny, oligarchy, or confusion.&#8221;</p><footer>Preface to the Frame of Government of Pennsylvania (1682) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/17th_century/pa04.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…for liberty without obedience is confusion, and obedience without liberty is slavery.&#8221;</p><footer>Preface to the Frame of Government of Pennsylvania (1682) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://avalon.law.yale.edu/17th_century/pa04.asp" rel="noopener">The Avalon Project</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
